 because their conditional distributions are similar.","The marginal distributions must each be 50%-50%."],"e":"When conditional distributions across groups are similar, the data show little evidence of association. Causation cannot be inferred from a two-way observational summary, and marginal distributions need not be equal."},{"unit":"U2","topicCode":"2.2","skill":"3.B","q":"Among 300 survey respondents, 90 are renters and support a proposal, 60 are renters and oppose it, 75 are homeowners and support it, and 75 are homeowners and oppose it. What is the joint relative frequency of being a renter who supports the proposal?","correct":"0.30","distractors":["135/300 = 0.45","90/150 = 0.60","150/300 = 0.50"],"e":"A joint relative frequency uses the grand total: 90/300 = 0.30. Values such as 90/150 = 0.60 are conditional on renter status rather than joint."},{"unit":"U2","topicCode":"2.6","skill":"3.C","q":"Suppose P(A ∩ B) = 0.18 and P(B) = 0.30. What is P(A | B)?","correct":"0.60","distractors":["0.18(0.30)=0.054","0.30−0.18=0.12","0.30/0.12=2.50"],"e":"Conditional probability is P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B)/P(B) = 0.18/0.30 = 0.60. Multiplying rather than dividing would answer a different probability question."},{"unit":"U2","topicCode":"2.3","skill":"3.C","q":"A simulation uses digits 0–9, with 0–2 representing a defective item and 3–9 representing a nondefective item. Five digits form one simulated shipment. What event should be counted to estimate the probability a shipment has at least two defective items?","correct":"Trials in which at least two of the five digits are 0, 1, or 2","distractors":["Trials in which exactly two digits are 0, 1, or 2","Trials in which the first two digits are both 0, 1, or 2","Trials in which at least two digits are 3 through 9"],"e":"Digits 0–2 model defect with probability 0.3, and a five-digit trial models five items. 'At least two' includes two, three, four, or five defective outcomes, not only exactly two."},{"unit":"U2","topicCode":"2.4","skill":"3.C","q":"A fair six-sided die is rolled twice. What is the probability that at least one roll is a 6?","correct":"11/36","distractors":["1/6","1/36","(5/6)^2 = 25/36"],"e":"Use the complement: P(at least one 6) = 1 − P(no 6 on either roll) = 1 − (5/6)^2 = 11/36. The 1/36 value is the probability of two sixes."},{"unit":"U2","topicCode":"2.4","skill":"3.C","q":"An event E has probability 0.27. What is the probability that E does not occur?","correct":"0.73","distractors":["P(E)=0.27","1−0.63=0.37","1+0.27=1.27"],"e":"An event and its complement exhaust the sample space, so their probabilities sum to 1. Thus P(Eᶜ) = 1 − 0.27 = 0.73."},{"unit":"U2","topicCode":"2.5","skill":"4.B","q":"For events A and B, P(A) = 0.40, P(B) = 0.35, and P(A ∩ B) = 0. Which statement is correct?","correct":"A and B are mutually exclusive.","distractors":["A and B are independent.","P(A ∪ B) = 0.14.","A and B must have equal probabilities."],"e":"Events are mutually exclusive when they cannot occur together, so P(A ∩ B)=0. Because both events have positive probability, independence would require P(A ∩ B)=0.40(0.35)=0.14, not zero."},{"unit":"U2","topicCode":"2.5","skill":"3.C","q":"If P(A) = 0.55, P(B) = 0.30, and A and B are mutually exclusive, what is P(A ∪ B)?","correct":"0.85","distractors":["0.55−0.30=0.25","0.55(0.30)=0.165","1.00 because the events are disjoint"],"e":"For mutually exclusive events, the intersection probability is zero. Therefore P(A ∪ B) = P(A)+P(B) = 0.55+0.30 = 0.85."},{"unit":"U2","topicCode":"2.7","skill":"3.C","q":"Events A and B are independent with P(A)=0.60 and P(B)=0.25. What is P(A ∩ B)?","correct":"0.15","distractors":["0.60+0.25=0.85","0.60−0.25=0.35","0.60/0.25=2.40"],"e":"For independent events, P(A ∩ B)=P(A)P(B)=0.60(0.25)=0.15. Adding the probabilities would not compute the intersection."},{"unit":"U2","topicCode":"2.7","skill":"3.C","q":"Suppose P(A)=0.50, P(B)=0.40, and P(A ∩ B)=0.10. What is P(A ∪ B)?","correct":"0.80","distractors":["0.50+0.40=0.90","0.50−0.40+0.10=0.20","0.50"],"e":"The general addition rule gives P(A ∪ B)=0.50+0.40−0.10=0.80. The intersection must be subtracted because it is included in both marginal probabilities."},{"unit":"U2","topicCode":"2.8","skill":"3.A","q":"For the device-failure model, which statement verifies that the table is a valid discrete probability distribution?","correct":"Probabilities are all in [0,1] and sum to 1.","distractors":["The x-values add to 6 and the probabilities decrease a